@prefix : <http://purl.org/edu/bloom#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xml: <http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix bloom: <http://purl.org/edu/bloom#> .
@prefix cs2023: <http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@base <http://purl.org/edu/bloom#> .

<http://purl.org/edu/bloom> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                             owl:versionIRI <http://purl.org/edu/bloom/2.0.0> ;
                             rdfs:comment "Represents framework-specific skill levels as individuals, their ordinal organization, and the classes and properties used to describe action-verb vocabularies. Verb and verb-type individuals are maintained in separate population files."@en ;
                             rdfs:label "Skill-Level and Action-Verb Ontology for OntoKSD"@en .

#################################################################
#    Annotation properties
#################################################################

###  http://purl.org/dc/terms/references
dcterms:references rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel
skos:altLabel rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition
skos:definition rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example
skos:example rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note
skos:note rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel
skos:prefLabel rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty .


#################################################################
#    Object Properties
#################################################################

###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#directFollows
bloom:directFollows rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                    owl:inverseOf bloom:directPrecedes ;
                    rdf:type owl:FunctionalProperty ;
                    rdfs:domain bloom:SkillLevel ;
                    rdfs:range bloom:SkillLevel ;
                    rdfs:comment """
Relates a SkillLevel individual to its immediate predecessor in
the ordering adopted within the same reference scheme.

directFollows is the inverse of directPrecedes. For example,
Applying directly follows Understanding in the Bloom-based scheme.

The property is functional because each level has at most one
immediate predecessor under the adopted linear-order policy.
As the inverse of the asymmetric and irreflexive directPrecedes,
it also excludes self-links and reciprocal predecessor links.

The property is not declared transitive: if C directly follows B
and B directly follows A, A is an indirect predecessor of C.
Longer chains and cycles involving three or more levels require
property-path queries or separate validation checks.

The relation expresses category ordering, not mandatory instructional
prerequisites, measured cognitive differences, or learner progression.
"""@en ;
                    rdfs:label "directly follows"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#directPrecedes
bloom:directPrecedes rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ,
                              owl:FunctionalProperty ,
                              owl:AsymmetricProperty ,
                              owl:IrreflexiveProperty ;
                     rdfs:domain bloom:SkillLevel ;
                     rdfs:range bloom:SkillLevel ;
                     skos:definition """Relates a SkillLevel individual to its immediate successor in the ordering adopted within the same reference scheme.

directPrecedes identifies the immediate successor; its inverse, directFollows, identifies the immediate predecessor. These relations organize level categories rather than occurrences of instruction or learner development.

Under the adopted linear-order policy, both properties are functional, allowing at most one immediate successor and one immediate predecessor per level. The relation is irreflexive because a level cannot immediately precede itself, and asymmetric because two levels cannot immediately precede each other.

Neither property is declared transitive: if A directly precedes B and B directly precedes C, C is an indirect successor of A, not its immediate successor. Property-path queries can retrieve longer chains and detect cycles involving three or more levels, which asymmetry and irreflexivity alone do not exclude.

Ordering applies within a scheme. It does not establish correspondence between schemes, measurable differences in cognitive demand, mandatory instructional prerequisites, or learner progression.""" .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#hasActionVerb
bloom:hasActionVerb rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                    owl:inverseOf bloom:isActionVerbFor ;
                    rdfs:domain bloom:SkillLevel ;
                    rdfs:range bloom:ActionVerb ;
                    rdfs:comment """
Associates a SkillLevel individual with an ActionVerb whose use is
associated with that category under an identifiable source or
contextual justification.

hasActionVerb relates the level to the vocabulary entry;
isActionVerbFor expresses the inverse direction. The association
supports retrieval of expressions that may help formulate or interpret
performance requirements.

The property is not functional because a level may be associated
with multiple verbs. Its inverse is also not functional because
a verb may be associated with multiple levels.

The association does not determine the cognitive demand of every
use of the expression. Interpretation requires examining the task,
knowledge, expected product, and applicable conditions. The property
does not require every level to have a populated verb list.
"""@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#hasActionVerbType
bloom:hasActionVerbType rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                        owl:inverseOf bloom:isActionVerbTypeFor ;
                        rdfs:domain bloom:ActionVerb ;
                        rdfs:range bloom:ActionVerbType ;
                        rdfs:comment """
Associates an ActionVerb with an ActionVerbType that classifies
its pedagogical, disciplinary, or framework-related scope.

hasActionVerbType relates the vocabulary entry to its category;
isActionVerbTypeFor expresses the inverse direction.

The property is not functional because categories may overlap:
an expression may be both computing-oriented and listed in a
particular curricular framework. Multiple verbs may share a type.

Classification supports vocabulary organization and filtering.
It does not assign a SkillLevel, establish that all uses of the verb
have the same cognitive demand, or replace provenance for a particular
verb–level association.
"""@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#isActionVerbFor
bloom:isActionVerbFor rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                      rdfs:domain bloom:ActionVerb ;
                      rdfs:range bloom:SkillLevel ;
                      rdfs:comment """
Associates an ActionVerb with a SkillLevel individual for which
the expression is listed or explicitly justified as a candidate
description of expected performance.

This property is the inverse of hasActionVerb. It allows a vocabulary
entry to identify multiple associated levels, including categories
from different reference schemes where separately supported.

The property is not functional because the interpretation of an
expression may vary with its source, sense, and task context.
Multiple associations do not establish equivalence between their
target levels.

Each association should retain its supporting source or contextual
justification. The relation does not automatically classify a task,
assign a required level to a KnowledgeSkill pairing, or attribute
proficiency to a learner.
"""@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#isActionVerbTypeFor
bloom:isActionVerbTypeFor rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                          rdfs:domain bloom:ActionVerbType ;
                          rdfs:range bloom:ActionVerb ;
                          rdfs:comment """
Associates an ActionVerbType with an ActionVerb classified under
that category.

This property is the inverse of hasActionVerbType and supports
retrieval of vocabulary entries by pedagogical, disciplinary,
or framework-related scope.

The property is not functional because a category may classify
multiple verbs. Category membership is not exclusive unless additional
restrictions explicitly establish exclusivity.

The association concerns vocabulary classification rather than
cognitive-level assignment. It does not independently establish
the source or validity of any verb–level association.
"""@en .


#################################################################
#    Data properties
#################################################################

###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#hasOrdinalPosition
bloom:hasOrdinalPosition rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ,
                                  owl:FunctionalProperty ;
                         rdfs:domain bloom:SkillLevel ;
                         rdfs:range xsd:int ;
                         rdfs:comment """Associates a SkillLevel individual with an integer representing its ordinal position within the adopted reference scheme.

hasOrdinalPosition supports sorting, filtering, and retrieval of level categories. Values are interpreted within their scheme: identical positions across schemes do not establish equivalent categories, and differences between positions do not measure distances in cognitive demand.

The property is functional because each level occupies a single position within its scheme. Functionality does not require different levels to have different positions; uniqueness and consistency with directPrecedes require separate checks.

An ordinal position does not establish mandatory instructional prerequisites, learner progression, or attained proficiency.""" .


#################################################################
#    Classes
#################################################################

###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#ActionVerb
bloom:ActionVerb rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                   owl:onProperty bloom:hasActionVerbType ;
                                   owl:someValuesFrom bloom:ActionVerbType
                                 ] ,
                                 [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                   owl:onProperty bloom:isActionVerbFor ;
                                   owl:someValuesFrom bloom:SkillLevel
                                 ] ;
                 owl:disjointWith bloom:ActionVerbType ,
                                  bloom:SkillLevel ;
                 rdfs:comment """A lexical action expression used to describe expected performance in a competence specification or learning outcome.

In OntoKSD, ActionVerb represents an identifiable vocabulary entry that can be classified through hasActionVerbType and associated with one or more SkillLevel individuals through isActionVerbFor. A KnowledgeSkill pairing may reference a verb through hasBloomLevelJustificationVerb to document the wording supporting its required-level assignment.

Verb–level associations express source-based or explicitly justified interpretations. A verb alone does not determine cognitive demand: its meaning depends on the knowledge involved, expected product, task conditions, and assessment criteria. The same expression may therefore be associated with different levels without implying equivalence between those levels.

ActionVerb is distinct from SkillLevel, which qualifies expected performance, and from Skill, which specifies a capability. Its instances are maintained in separate vocabulary files, allowing lexical content to evolve independently of the core ontology.""" .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#ActionVerbType
bloom:ActionVerbType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     owl:disjointWith bloom:SkillLevel ;
                     rdfs:comment """A controlled category used to classify action-verb vocabulary entries according to their pedagogical, disciplinary, or framework-related scope.

In OntoKSD, ActionVerbType individuals are associated with ActionVerb through hasActionVerbType. Categories may distinguish general educational expressions, computing-oriented expressions, and expressions listed in a particular curricular framework.

These classifications are not necessarily exclusive: the same verb may belong to multiple categories. They support vocabulary organization, filtering, and reuse without determining the verb’s associated SkillLevel.

Category membership does not replace provenance for a particular verb–level association. Such associations require their own source or contextual justification.

ActionVerbType is distinct from ActionVerb and SkillLevel. Its individuals are maintained in separate vocabulary files.""" .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#CC2020SkillLevel
bloom:CC2020SkillLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf bloom:SkillLevel ,
                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                         owl:onProperty bloom:directFollows ;
                                         owl:allValuesFrom bloom:CC2020SkillLevel
                                       ] ,
                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                         owl:onProperty bloom:directPrecedes ;
                                         owl:allValuesFrom bloom:CC2020SkillLevel
                                       ] ,
                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                         owl:onProperty bloom:hasOrdinalPosition ;
                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                         owl:onDataRange xsd:int
                                       ] ;
                       owl:disjointWith cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel ;
                       rdfs:comment """A SkillLevel category belonging to the revised Bloom cognitive-process scheme used in CC2020-oriented OntoKSD specifications.

CC2020SkillLevel has six named individuals: Remembering, Understanding, Applying, Analyzing, Evaluating, and Creating. These individuals qualify the performance expected for a knowledge element through a KnowledgeSkill pairing. They are not subclasses or learner-specific proficiency judgments.

The class name identifies the scheme’s use in CC2020-oriented specifications; it does not imply that CC2020 originated Bloom’s taxonomy.

Ordinal positions and ordering relations organize the categories within the scheme. They do not establish equal distances in cognitive demand or a mandatory instructional sequence.

CC2020SkillLevel is disjoint from CS2023SkillLevel because their individuals denote categories belonging to distinct reference schemes. Correspondence between their categories requires explicit justification and does not establish identity.""" .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#SkillLevel
bloom:SkillLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:comment """A controlled category used to qualify the expected performance required by a competence specification within an identifiable reference scheme.

In OntoKSD, SkillLevel provides the common superclass for CC2020SkillLevel and CS2023SkillLevel. Specific levels, such as Applying and Develop, are represented as individuals of their respective scheme classes, rather than as classes themselves.

A KnowledgeSkill pairing identifies its required level through hasSkillLevel, allowing the same knowledge element to participate in requirements with different performance expectations.

SkillLevel is distinct from Skill, which represents a capability constituent, and from ProficiencyLevel, which expresses an interpretation of assessed performance or a curricular proficiency expectation. Assigning a required SkillLevel does not establish that a learner has demonstrated it.

Levels may have an ordinal position and explicit ordering relations within their scheme. These support organization and retrieval without establishing measurable distances, equivalence across schemes, or a mandatory learning sequence.

This shared abstraction supports consistent references and queries across schemes while preserving their distinct categories and interpretations.""" .


###  http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#CS2023SkillLevel
cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
                        rdfs:subClassOf bloom:SkillLevel ,
                                        [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                          owl:onProperty bloom:directFollows ;
                                          owl:allValuesFrom cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel
                                        ] ,
                                        [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                          owl:onProperty bloom:directPrecedes ;
                                          owl:allValuesFrom cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel
                                        ] ,
                                        [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                          owl:onProperty bloom:hasOrdinalPosition ;
                                          owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                          owl:onDataRange xsd:int
                                        ] ;
                        rdfs:comment """A SkillLevel category belonging to the four-level scheme adopted in CS2023 to qualify expected performance in computing.

CS2023SkillLevel has four named individuals: Explain, Apply, Evaluate, and Develop. In OntoKSD, these individuals qualify the performance required for a knowledge element through a KnowledgeSkill pairing. They are not subclasses, explicit Skill constituents, or learner-specific proficiency judgments.

The CS2023 categories are loosely aligned with revised Bloom’s taxonomy. This alignment supports comparison without making similarly named categories identical or interchangeable.

Ordinal positions and ordering relations organize the categories within the adopted representation. They do not measure distances in cognitive demand or prescribe a complete sequence of learning prerequisites.

CS2023SkillLevel specializes SkillLevel and is disjoint from CC2020SkillLevel, preserving the identity of each framework’s categories while supporting shared properties and queries.""" .


#################################################################
#    Individuals
#################################################################

###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#Analyzing
bloom:Analyzing rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                         bloom:CC2020SkillLevel ;
                bloom:directPrecedes bloom:Evaluating ;
                bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 4 ;
                rdfs:comment """
A cognitive-process category concerning the examination of parts and
their relationships to one another and to an overall structure or purpose.

Analyzing qualifies requirements involving differentiation, organization,
or attribution, such as examining how the components of an algorithm
contribute to its behavior.

In OntoKSD, this individual identifies an expected performance category
within the CC2020-oriented scheme. Its ordinal position does not measure
cognitive difficulty or establish learner attainment.
"""@en ;
                rdfs:label "Analyzing"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#Applying
bloom:Applying rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        bloom:CC2020SkillLevel ;
               bloom:directPrecedes bloom:Analyzing ;
               bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 3 ;
               rdfs:comment """
A cognitive-process category concerning the execution or implementation
of a procedure in a given situation.

Applying qualifies requirements in which knowledge is mobilized through
a method, technique, or operation, such as executing a specified algorithm
or using a procedure to solve a problem. The required interpretation
depends on the task and its conditions, rather than on an action verb alone.

In OntoKSD, this individual qualifies a KnowledgeSkill requirement.
It is distinct from the CS2023 category Apply and from an attributed
proficiency level.
"""@en ;
               rdfs:label "Applying"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#Creating
bloom:Creating rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        bloom:CC2020SkillLevel ;
               bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 6 ;
               rdfs:comment """
A cognitive-process category concerning the organization of elements
into a coherent or functional whole through generating, planning,
or producing.

Creating qualifies requirements involving the formulation or construction
of a solution or artifact, such as designing a computational model
to address stated requirements. It does not require that the result
be unprecedented beyond the educational context.

In OntoKSD, this individual identifies required performance rather
than evidence that a learner has produced an adequate result.
Its final position in the adopted ordering does not prescribe an
instructional sequence.
"""@en ;
               rdfs:label "Creating"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#Evaluating
bloom:Evaluating rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          bloom:CC2020SkillLevel ;
                 bloom:directPrecedes bloom:Creating ;
                 bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 5 ;
                 rdfs:comment """
A cognitive-process category concerning judgments made against
identifiable criteria and standards.

Evaluating qualifies requirements involving checking or critiquing,
such as assessing whether a proposed solution satisfies correctness,
security, or efficiency criteria. The criteria distinguish evaluative
judgment from unsupported preference.

In OntoKSD, this individual qualifies expected performance within
the CC2020-oriented scheme. It is distinct from the CS2023 category
Evaluate and does not itself represent an assessment judgment.
"""@en ;
                 rdfs:label "Evaluating"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#Remembering
bloom:Remembering rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           bloom:CC2020SkillLevel ;
                  bloom:directPrecedes bloom:Understanding ;
                  bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 1 ;
                  rdfs:comment """
A cognitive-process category concerning the recognition or recall
of relevant knowledge from memory.

Remembering qualifies requirements involving the retrieval of facts,
terms, conventions, or previously learned procedures, such as recalling
the defining components of a deterministic finite automaton.

In OntoKSD, this individual specifies an expected cognitive process.
Its first position in the adopted scheme does not establish that
instruction must begin with remembering tasks or that recall alone
demonstrates a competence.
"""@en ;
                  rdfs:label "Remembering"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/bloom#Understanding
bloom:Understanding rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             bloom:CC2020SkillLevel ;
                    bloom:directPrecedes bloom:Applying ;
                    bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 2 ;
                    rdfs:comment """
A cognitive-process category concerning the construction of meaning
from verbal, written, graphical, or other representations.

Understanding qualifies requirements involving interpretation,
exemplification, classification, summarization, inference, comparison,
or explanation, such as explaining the behavior represented by an
automaton diagram.

In OntoKSD, this individual identifies the required treatment of
knowledge within a specification. It is not an assertion that a
particular learner understands that knowledge.
"""@en ;
                    rdfs:label "Understanding"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#Apply
cs2023:Apply rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                      cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel ;
             bloom:directPrecedes cs2023:Evaluate ;
             bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 2 ;
             rdfs:comment """
A CS2023 skill-level category concerning the use of knowledge,
procedures, or techniques to carry out a task.

Apply qualifies requirements such as configuring a system, interpreting
an output, or testing a solution, where the intended demand is
established by the task and its conditions. These examples do not
determine the category independently of context.

In OntoKSD, Apply is a controlled value for CS2023-oriented
KnowledgeSkill requirements. It is distinct from bloom:Applying;
similar terminology does not establish category identity.
"""@en ;
             rdfs:label "Apply"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#Develop
cs2023:Develop rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel ;
               bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 4 ;
               rdfs:comment """
A CS2023 skill-level category concerning the formulation, construction,
integration, or revision of a solution or artifact.

Develop qualifies requirements involving activities such as designing,
composing, refactoring, or producing, interpreted against the expected
result and task scope. It is not limited to writing source code.

In OntoKSD, this individual qualifies required performance in the
CS2023 scheme. Its ordinal position is a representation convention,
not evidence of proficiency or a requirement that instruction follow
all preceding categories.
"""@en ;
               rdfs:label "Develop"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#Evaluate
cs2023:Evaluate rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                         cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel ;
                bloom:directPrecedes cs2023:Develop ;
                bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 3 ;
                rdfs:comment """
A CS2023 skill-level category concerning the examination, comparison,
and judgment of information, alternatives, or solutions.

Evaluate qualifies requirements involving analytical distinctions
or justified decisions, such as comparing design alternatives against
stated criteria. Its interpretation follows the CS2023 scheme rather
than an assumed identity with a single Bloom category.

In OntoKSD, this individual identifies expected performance.
It is distinct from bloom:Evaluating and from the learner-specific
judgment through which that performance may later be assessed.
"""@en ;
                rdfs:label "Evaluate"@en .


###  http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#Explain
cs2023:Explain rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        cs2023:CS2023SkillLevel ;
               bloom:directPrecedes cs2023:Apply ;
               bloom:hasOrdinalPosition 1 ;
               rdfs:comment """
A CS2023 skill-level category concerning the articulation of relevant
knowledge through descriptions, explanations, summaries, or related
forms of expression.

Explain qualifies requirements such as describing a concept,
summarizing a process, or explaining system behavior. Its scope
follows the CS2023 category, which is loosely aligned with the
Remembering and Understanding categories of revised Bloom's taxonomy.

In OntoKSD, this individual is a level value rather than an ActionVerb
entry. The lexical expression explain may be represented separately
in the action-verb vocabulary.
"""@en ;
               rdfs:label "Explain"@en .


